Schema migrations
=================

Run from the backend directory; the config path is explicit because
alembic.ini lives here rather than at the repository root:

    alembic -c alembic.ini current          # where this database is
    alembic -c alembic.ini upgrade head     # apply everything pending
    alembic -c alembic.ini downgrade -1     # step back one revision

The DSN is not in alembic.ini. env.py takes it from app.core.config, so
DB_TARGET, DATABASE_URL and the Aiven/Neon URIs select the database here
exactly as they do at runtime. Two URLs would be two sources of truth,
and the failure is migrating one database while the app reads another.


Adopting an existing database
-----------------------------

    alembic -c alembic.ini stamp head

The first revision is deliberately empty. The schema predates Alembic --
it was built by create_all -- so the baseline records where that schema
got to rather than recreating it.


Autogenerate: read the diff before trusting it
----------------------------------------------

    alembic -c alembic.ini revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"

Always open the generated file. Autogenerate compares the models against
the database, and several objects here exist only in the database:

  - ix_chunk_embedding_hnsw   HNSW index behind vector search
  - ix_chunk_content_fts      GIN index behind keyword search
  - composite foreign keys    (topic_id, subject_id) and similar, which
                              keep a question's topic inside its subject

Autogenerate does not know they are intentional and will emit DROP for
each one. Run against the current models it produced 33 of them. Such a
migration applies cleanly, reports success, and leaves semantic search
quietly broken -- delete those lines before committing.

Check for them specifically:

    grep -nE "drop_index|drop_constraint|drop_table|drop_column" \
      migrations/versions/<new_file>.py


create_all still runs at startup
--------------------------------

init_database() creates any missing tables, which is what makes a fresh
clone work with no migration step. It does not add columns to tables that
already exist, so it cannot bring an older database up to date -- that is
what these migrations are for. A new database is created by create_all
and then stamped; an existing one is upgraded.
